Z||000011||BAM-BAM||03-12-2000||12:35 PM||||  Rodney, Your OEM gears, will they last longer with a different grease used in the housing? I rebuilt my right headlight motor last week. I thought I paid attention and marked everything ok, but when I installed it and turned it on it went about a quarter of the way up and hit "THAT SNAG" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. It jups over it ok when the lid is up but if the lid is down it won't push the headlight door up. How much are the replacement gears?<P> Bam <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000012||Rodney||03-12-2000||01:57 PM||||I supply a packet of Dow Corning 33 Medium silicone grease with each gear. It is $2 an ounce! Original assemblies used a hydrocarbon based lubricant. In the heat the lubricant turned to a thick non lubricating paste. The silicone will not. I believe they did that because the silicone is much more expensive. So with the silicone grease the gears should last longer than the originals. <P>My gears are $21.50 each and $1.50 shipping. Includes the gear and 2 internal bumpers. Also the silicone grease and screws and nuts to replace the rivits you need to drill out to open the case. All OEM parts. Not aftermarket! Brush/contact assemblies are $21.00 each. Shipping is $2.00.<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
